<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/" type="topic" id="canvas-editing">
  <info><link type="guide" xref="canvas"/><desc>Draw, Erase, and Crop in coyote.</desc></info>
  <title>Draw, Erase, and Crop</title>
  <p>Choose <gui>File</gui> → <gui>New</gui> for a blank drawing, <gui>Import...</gui> for PNG, JPEG, or static SVG, or <gui>Edit</gui> → <gui>Paste</gui> for a PNG or JPEG offered by the clipboard. Select the <gui>Pencil</gui>, <gui>Eraser</gui>, or <gui>Crop</gui> icon below the menu bar, or use the matching <gui>Edit</gui> menu item. The active icon is highlighted. Hover over an icon to see its name. <gui>Pencil</gui> is selected by default.</p>
  <p>Selecting a single saved revision or choosing <gui>Show in Canvas</gui> displays that exact saved image; it does not replace or discard a working draft. When the displayed saved revision is writable and <gui>View</gui> → <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on, <gui>Pencil</gui>, <gui>Eraser</gui>, and <gui>Crop</gui> are available. There is no separate Edit Revision command. A completed pointer or finger stroke, or <gui>Apply Crop</gui>, begins a working copy based on the displayed revision. If the previous working copy has unsaved changes, Canvas preserves them in a new revision before switching. Starting or canceling a gesture without completing it does not change drafts.</p>
  <p>Use the <gui>Pencil color</gui> swatch in the toolbar to choose an opaque color for new mouse, pen, or finger Pencil strokes. It starts at near-black for each session. Changing the swatch does not recolor existing ink or switch tools. Saved revisions and attachments retain the colors drawn.</p>
  <p>Draw with the pen tip or mouse. Select <gui>Eraser</gui> in the toolbar or <gui>Edit</gui> menu to remove ink with a 16-source-pixel brush without changing the background. Ink disappears in the preview as you drag; a recognized tablet eraser tip behaves the same without changing the selected tool. Selected <gui>Crop</gui> takes precedence. The preview removes ink only: saved revisions and attachments are unchanged until release commits the whole gesture as one <gui>Undo</gui>/<gui>Redo</gui> edit. Press <key>Escape</key> to cancel and restore the ink without an edit or revision change. If live preview is unavailable, Canvas shows the unchanged drawing and brush guide. Brush and guide stay 16 source pixels wide regardless of zoom.</p>
  <p>For touchscreen editing, enable <guiseq><gui>Options</gui><gui>Edit with Finger</gui></guiseq>. It is off at the start of each session. With <gui>Pencil</gui> or <gui>Eraser</gui> selected, drag one finger on a writable drawing while <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on. The operation is fixed when the contact starts: <gui>Pencil</gui> paints a 3-source-pixel stroke; <gui>Eraser</gui> removes ink only with a 16-source-pixel brush and leaves the background unchanged. Ink removal is visible while you drag; lift commits one undoable edit, while Escape cancels and restores ink without changing the revision. Widths stay constant at different zoom levels. Extra contacts do not draw or take over. Touch panning remains available when the option is off or touch editing is otherwise ineligible, including read-only drawings, hidden markup, and <gui>Crop</gui> or Zoom modes. The setting is session-local and does not affect mouse or pen input. You can edit a displayed saved revision directly; the first completed gesture starts a working copy from that revision.</p>
  <p>Select <gui>Crop</gui> in the toolbar or <gui>Edit</gui> menu, then drag the rectangle with the pen or mouse or enter exact pixel bounds. The editing tools remain available when the displayed drawing is writable and <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on; they are disabled when no drawing is displayed, it is not writable, or <gui>Show Markup</gui> is off. A pending crop on a saved revision is only a proposal: <gui>Cancel</gui> or <key>Escape</key> leaves the revision and all drafts unchanged. Only <gui>Apply Crop</gui> commits the crop to a working copy of the displayed revision. Switching to <gui>Pencil</gui> or <gui>Eraser</gui> discards the pending crop. <gui>Apply Crop</gui>, <gui>Cancel</gui>, and <key>Escape</key> return to the drawing tool selected before <gui>Crop</gui>. Crop does not resample pixels, and zoom changes only the view. No new shortcuts are assigned; Tab and arrow keys use native toolbar navigation, and Edit menu mnemonics remain available.</p>
  <p>Select the <gui>Zoom In</gui> (+) or <gui>Zoom Out</gui> (−) magnifier, then click or tap the drawing to zoom at that point. Press <key>Escape</key> to return to the previous editing tool without undoing zoom. A pending crop stays in place. You can also choose <gui>Zoom In</gui> or <gui>Zoom Out</gui> from the <gui>View</gui> menu for an immediate step around the center of the view.</p>
  <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Fit</gui></guiseq> to show the full drawing in the current viewport, or <guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Actual Size</gui></guiseq> for 100%. Fit works on the working copy and saved revisions, and it preserves any pending crop. It changes only the display scale, not the drawing or its attachments.</p>
</page>
